  author={Yin, De and Zhu, Lei and Zhang, Xiao},
  journal={IEEE Transactions on Antennas and Propagation}, 
  title={A Design Method for Reflective Linear-to-Circular Polarization Converter With Customizable Axial Ratio Bandwidth Based on Multimode Resonators}, 
  year={2026},
  volume={74},
  number={5},
  pages={4374-4388},
  abstract={The 3-dB-axial ratio (AR) fractional bandwidth (FBW ${}_{3\text {dB}-AR}$ ) of conventional linear-to-circular polarization converters (LCPCs) is typically limited by the intrinsic characteristics of the structure. This article presents a novel approach for a class of reflective LCPCs with customizable FBW ${}_{3\textrm {dB}-AR}$ . The proposed LCPC element consists of a dual-polarized multimode resonator (MMR) and an amplitude/phase regulator (APR). The APR effectively converts incident linearly polarized (LP) waves into reflected circularly polarized (CP) waves. The FBW ${}_{3\textrm {dB}-AR}$  of the LCPC element is determined by the reflection coefficient of MMR (RC ${}_{MMR}$ ) and APR (RC ${}_{APR}$ ). By properly designing RC ${}_{MMR}$  and RC ${}_{APR}$ , the FBW ${}_{3\textrm {dB}-AR}$  of LCPCs can be customized. Using bandpass filter synthesis methodologies, four LCPC elements with FBW ${}_{3\textrm {dB}-AR}$  of 0.8%, 10%, 40%, and 80% are designed based on 2-D transverse and 3-D longitudinal MMRs. The simulated results match well with the design specifications. Ultimately, two representative LCPC cases with FBW ${}_{3\textrm {dB}-AR}$  of 10% (4.75–5.25 GHz) and 80% (3–7 GHz) are fabricated and measured to validate the proposed design technique. The measured FBW ${}_{3\textrm {dB}-AR}$  of Cases I and II are 9.4% (4.78–5.25 GHz) and 80.4% (2.99–8.01 GHz) within ±40° incidences, respectively, matching well with both simulated and theoretical predictions. Given the advantages of customizable AR bandwidth and high selectivity, the proposed design technique is a promising candidate for reflective LCPCs to manipulate the polarization states of electromagnetic (EM) waves.},
  keywords={Bandwidth;Freeports;Transmission line matrix methods;Topology;Regulators;Impedance;Design methodology;Antennas;Reflection coefficient;Power transmission lines;Amplitude/phase regulator (APR);bandpass filters;linear-to-circular polarization conversion;multimode resonators (MMRs)},
